WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_whf_121'), 'The mobile app sends the same request twice because the learner double-taps "Leave course":

DELETE /v1/enrollments/42  ->  HTTP/2 204
DELETE /v1/enrollments/42  ->  HTTP/2 404  {"error":"Enrollment not found"}

After both calls, enrollment 42 no longer exists and nothing else changed.

Statement: "Because the second call returned a different status code, DELETE is not idempotent on this API."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Idempotent means that sending the request several times has the same effect on the server as sending it once. After one call or two, enrollment 42 is gone and nothing else changed, so the method is idempotent. The response is allowed to differ: there is nothing left to delete the second time, so 404 is a correct answer. The misconception is that idempotency promises identical responses; it promises identical server state.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_whf_121') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
