09:14:03.120 POST /submissions -> 401 {"error":"invalid_token"}
09:14:03.161 POST /submissions -> 401 {"error":"invalid_token"}
09:14:03.198 POST /submissions -> 401 {"error":"invalid_token"}
09:14:03.240 POST /submissions -> 401 {"error":"invalid_token"}
09:14:03.277 POST /submissions -> 401 {"error":"invalid_token"}
09:14:03.315 POST /submissions -> 401 {"error":"invalid_token"}

Statement: "Waiting longer between these attempts, with exponential backoff, would have fixed the incident."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Backoff only spreads the same six requests over a longer time; each one still carries the expired token, so each one still gets 401. A 401 describes the request (its credentials), not a temporary state of the server: the fix is to stop retrying and get a new token or send the user to sign in again. Backoff is the right tool only for failures that can go away on their own, such as a network error, 429, 502, 503 or 504. The common misconception is that backoff makes any retry safe; it makes a retryable failure cheaper, it does not make a non-retryable one succeed.', 1, 5
