VALUES ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_whf_312'), 1, 'For each attempt, ask two questions in order: did fetch throw or return a response, and if it failed, could the very same request succeed a moment later? Only then does the retry counter matter.'),
       ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_whf_312'), 2, 'Look at three things: the error''s name property (TimeoutError and TypeError are retryable, AbortError is not), response.status compared with a short list of retryable statuses, and response.headers.get("Retry-After"), which returns null when the header is missing. The loop needs an attempt number to compute the delay and to stop after retries + 1 calls.'),
       ((SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_coding_whf_312'), 3, 'A worked trace with retries 2 and baseDelayMs 10, when the server answers: network error, then 504, then 504. Call 1 rejects -> retry 1 -> sleep(10). Call 2 is 504 -> retry 2 -> sleep(20). Call 3 is 504 -> no retry left -> return that 504 response. If call 2 had been 504 with Retry-After: 3, the second sleep would be 3000 instead of 20; if call 1 had been a 404, it would be returned at once with no sleep at all.');
