    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_whf_341'), 'The mobile app enrolled learner u_4821 in a course on a slow network:

POST /enrollments HTTP/2
Host: api.codedev.test
content-type: application/json

{ "userId": "u_4821", "courseId": "web-http-fundamentals" }

The app gave up after 10 s without a response and sent exactly the same request again, which returned 201 Created. The database now has two enrollments for this learner and this course.

Statement: "Retrying a POST after a timeout is safe as long as the request body is identical, because the server then receives the same data twice."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. A timeout means the client does not know whether the first request was processed; here it was, and only the response was lost. POST asks the server to create a new resource, so each request that arrives creates another one - an identical body does not make it the same operation. RFC 9110 does not define POST as idempotent. To retry it safely, the client sends an Idempotency-Key that is the same for every retry of one user action, and the server returns the stored result for a key it has already seen; a unique constraint on (user_id, course_id) adds a second guard.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_whf_341') AND order_index = 1)
