The API stores exactly this representation for that learner and lesson, replacing any earlier one. The first attempt timed out.

Statement: "Sending this PUT a second time leaves the server in the same state as sending it once, so the client can retry it after the timeout."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. PUT means "make the resource at this URL have this state". The body carries the full new state, including the completion time chosen by the client, so applying it once or twice gives the same result. RFC 9110 defines PUT as idempotent for exactly this reason, and that is what makes automatic retries of PUT acceptable. The misconception to avoid is the opposite one: thinking idempotent means the responses are identical. A first PUT may return 201 and a second 200; only the effect on the server has to be the same.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_whf_341') AND order_index = 2)
